{"slots": {"question": [{"claim": "The paper asks whether online political hostility on X/Twitter is associated with political engagement and its antecedents rather than only with deprivation or personality-based explanations.", "quotes": [{"text": "We extend this revisionist perspective to online political hostility by asking whether hostile political expression on X/Twitter is associated not only with deprivation or personality-based explanations, but also with political engagement and its antecedents.", "page": 2}]}],
"central_claim": [{"claim": "Online political hostility is positively associated with indicators of political engagement and its antecedents, strongest for political interest, political knowledge and online participation, and hostile engaged users are more active with greater potential reach.", "quotes": [{"text": "Online political hostility is positively associated with these indicators, with the strongest associations for political interest, political knowledge, and online participation.", "page": 1}, {"text": "The greater engagement of hostile social media users may thus provide them with a higher reacher than other users.", "page": 1}]}],
"evidence": [{"claim": "The study combines two years of panel survey data from American X/Twitter users with scraped behavioral post data, with linked survey responses and millions of posts.", "quotes": [{"text": "We rely on a data infrastructure combining panel survey data with online behavioral data from X (see also [59]).", "page": 6}, {"text": "The total number of panelists where we were able to link survey responses to their X handle in this period was 2567.", "page": 6}, {"text": "During this period, we managed to scrape 9,250,180 posts from our panelists.", "page": 6}]}],
"key_findings": [{"claim": "Indicators of political engagement and its antecedents are positively associated with online political hostility.", "quotes": [{"text": "Fig 1 demonstrate positive and statistically significant associations between online political hostility and several indicators of political engagement and its antecedents (consistent with Expectation 1).", "page": 11}]}, {"claim": "The association is stronger for political interest and online participation, and is larger for the primary political hostility measure than for general sentiment or toxicity.", "quotes": [{"text": "The associations with online participation and political interest are, for example, 0.236 (95% CI [0.186; 0.285]) and 0.306 (95% CI [0.247; 0.366]) for online political hostility, whereas they are 0.176 (95% CI [0.103; 0.249]) and 0.170 (95% CI [0.086; 0.253]) for AFINN scores", "page": 11}]}, {"claim": "The associations are more pronounced among users with higher leader-directed affective polarization, especially for participation.", "quotes": [{"text": "The descriptive pattern suggests that the association between engagement and hostility is often stronger among more affectively polarized users: for those low on affective polarization only four out of eighteen associations, across our three measures of online hostility, are significant, whereas for those high on affective polarization fourteen out of eighteen are significant.", "page": 13}]}, {"claim": "More hostile users post more frequently and have more friends and followers, indicating greater activity and potential reach.", "quotes": [{"text": "The count-model results indicate that higher political hostility is associated with more posts and with larger numbers of followers and, to a lesser extent, friends.", "page": 15}, {"text": "A change from the mean level of hostility to 1.5 standard deviations above the mean is associated with an increase in the number of posts from around 420 to around 900 and an increase in the number of followers from around 220 to around 370.", "page": 15}]}],
"scope_and_limits": [{"claim": "The authors state the paper does not adjudicate between deprivation/personality accounts and does not establish causality.", "quotes": [{"text": "The present study does not seek to adjudicate between these literatures.", "page": 3}]}, {"claim": "The authors note they do not draw conclusions about formal network influence or centrality.", "quotes": [{"text": "Third, we examine whether politically hostile users are more active and have greater potential reach on X/Twitter, while avoiding claims about formal network influence or centrality.", "page": 3}]}, {"claim": "The findings are observational and may not generalize beyond American X/Twitter users.", "quotes": [{"text": "First, the data come from American X/Twitter users. This platform is a public, text-based, and highly political platform, and the associations observed here may not generalize directly to platforms with different affordances, audiences, moderation systems, or norms of interaction.", "page": 16}]}, {"claim": "The affective polarization measure captures leader-directed affect, not party or supporter affect.", "quotes": [{"text": "This measure captures affect toward party elites rather than affect toward parties as institutions or ordinary party supporters, as is standard in research on affective polarization.", "page": 9}]}],
"theoretical_positioning": [{"claim": "The authors place the work in classical political behavior and a revisionist view of engagement, extending the revisionist perspective to online political hostility.", "quotes": [{"text": "Classical research on political behavior emphasizes that education, internal efficacy, political interest, political knowledge, and participation are central to democratic engagement.", "page": 2}, {"text": "We extend this revisionist perspective to online political hostility by asking whether hostile political expression on X/Twitter is associated not only with deprivation or personality-based explanations, but also with political engagement and its antecedents.", "page": 2}, {"text": "Here, we extend and integrate the study of the negative side of political engagement into the study of online politics with a specific focus on online political hostility.", "page": 4}]}],
"interlocutor_claim": [{"claim": "The paper attributes to classical literature the claim that political engagement is a boon to democracy, which the authors qualify.", "quotes": [{"text": "Within the classical literature, political engagement has been seen as a boon to democracy.", "page": 4}, {"text": "At the same time, even in this classical literature, it was explicitly acknowledged that political engagement could go too far.", "page": 4}]}
]}}
